Q = [
dict(sec=7, img='B_rah', cap=CH,
stem="A 59-year-old woman with long-standing chronic bronchitis comes to the office for follow-up. She has smoked 40 pack-years. Her electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}The P-wave abnormality in lead II reflects enlargement of which of the following chambers?",
choices=["Both atria equally","Left atrium","Left ventricle","Right atrium","Right ventricle"], key=3,
exp="The P wave is the <b>right atrium depolarizing first and the left atrium second</b>. When the right atrium enlarges, the first half of the P wave grows and piles onto the second, so the P becomes <b>tall and peaked</b> (over 2.5 mm in lead II — \"P pulmonale\") without becoming wider. Chronic lung disease raises pulmonary pressures and strains the right heart. When the left atrium enlarges, the second half stretches out, making the P <b>wide</b> (over 120 ms, often notched — \"P mitrale\") with a deep terminal negative portion in V1.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right atrial enlargement makes the P wave tall and peaked in lead II (P pulmonale); left atrial enlargement makes it wide and notched (P mitrale).",
wrong={0:"Biatrial enlargement would make the P wave both tall and wide; hers has a normal duration.",
1:"Left atrial enlargement widens the P wave (over 120 ms) and deepens its terminal negative portion in V1; it does not make it tall.",
2:"Ventricular hypertrophy changes the QRS voltages, not the P wave.",
4:"Right ventricular hypertrophy changes the QRS (right axis, tall R in V1); the P-wave height reflects the atrium."},
eli5="The right atrium makes the first half of the P wave. When it gets big, that first half grows tall, so the P wave becomes a sharp peak."),

dict(sec=7, img=None,
stem="A 46-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis is in sinus rhythm. Her electrocardiogram shows a P wave in lead II lasting 140 ms with two humps, and a P wave in V1 with a small initial positive portion followed by a deep, wide negative portion.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the change in the P wave?",
choices=["Delayed activation of an enlarged left atrium","Ectopic atrial focus near the coronary sinus","Increased voltage from an enlarged right atrium","Slowed conduction through the atrioventricular node"], key=0,
exp="Mitral stenosis obstructs left atrial emptying, so the <b>left atrium enlarges</b>. The left atrium depolarizes <b>second</b>, so its enlargement prolongs the back half of the P wave: a <b>wide, notched P in lead II</b> (over 120 ms, \"P mitrale\"). Lead V1 sits in front of the right atrium and behind it lies the left atrium, so V1 sees the right atrium coming toward it (positive) and the left atrium moving away (negative); a large left atrium deepens and widens that <b>terminal negative portion</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Left atrial enlargement widens and notches the P wave in lead II and deepens its terminal negative component in V1.",
wrong={1:"An ectopic focus near the coronary sinus produces an inverted P wave in the inferior leads, not a wide notched one.",
2:"Right atrial enlargement makes the P tall and peaked in lead II and mostly positive in V1.",
3:"Atrioventricular nodal conduction affects the PR segment, not P-wave shape."},
eli5="The left atrium fires second, so it makes the back half of the P wave. When it's stretched big, that back half takes longer, and the P wave gets wide with two bumps."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_lvhlah', cap=CH,
stem="An 83-year-old man with severe aortic stenosis has the electrocardiogram shown. The axis is leftward. The S wave in V1 measures 24 mm and the R wave in V5 measures 28 mm.{FIG}Which of the following electrocardiographic criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy is the most specific?",
choices=["Downsloping ST depression with T-wave inversion laterally","Left axis deviation","P wave wider than 120 ms in lead II","R wave in aVL greater than 11 mm","S in V1 plus R in V5 or V6 of 35 mm or more"], key=3,
exp="Pressure overload from aortic stenosis causes concentric <b>left ventricular hypertrophy</b>. The lecture's criteria: a <b>left axis</b> (required) plus either voltage criterion. An <b>R wave in aVL over 11 mm is the most specific</b>; the <b>Sokolow–Lyon sum</b> (S in V1 or V2 + R in V5 or V6 ≥ 35 mm; here 24 + 28 = 52) is the <b>most sensitive</b>. The lateral ST depression and T-wave inversion (\"strain\") support hypertrophy but are easily mistaken for ischemia.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> For left ventricular hypertrophy, an R in aVL over 11 mm is the most specific criterion, and the Sokolow–Lyon sum (≥35 mm) the most sensitive; left axis is required by the lecture's rule.",
wrong={0:"The strain pattern supports hypertrophy but is nonspecific and can be caused by ischemia, drugs or electrolytes.",
1:"Left axis deviation is required by the lecture's rule but has many other causes, so it is not specific.",
2:"A wide P wave reflects left atrial enlargement, which accompanies but does not define ventricular hypertrophy.",
4:"The Sokolow–Lyon sum is the most sensitive voltage criterion, not the most specific."},
eli5="A thick left ventricle makes big electrical spikes. A tall spike in lead aVL is the most reliable single sign, while the chest-lead sum catches more cases but with more false alarms."),

dict(sec=7, img=None,
stem="A 67-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has an electrocardiogram before cataract surgery. He has no chest pain or shortness of breath and climbs two flights of stairs without symptoms. The tracing shows left axis deviation, an R wave in aVL of 14 mm, and 1-mm downsloping ST depression with asymmetric T-wave inversion in leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. The findings are unchanged from a tracing 2 years ago. High-sensitivity troponin is undetectable.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the ST-segment and T-wave changes?",
choices=["Acute subendocardial ischemia","Digoxin effect on repolarization","Old lateral myocardial infarction","Left ventricular strain pattern","Wellens pattern of anterior ischemia"], key=3,
exp="Left axis plus an R in aVL over 11 mm is <b>left ventricular hypertrophy</b>. Hypertrophy also alters repolarization in the lateral leads (I, aVL, V5–V6), producing <b>downsloping ST depression with asymmetric T-wave inversion — the \"strain\" pattern</b>. It is easy to mistake for ischemia, which is why the lecture stressed it. Here the changes are stable over 2 years, he has no symptoms, and troponin is undetectable.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Left ventricular hypertrophy can cause lateral ST depression and T-wave inversion (strain pattern) that mimics ischemia; compare with old tracings and the clinical picture.",
wrong={0:"Acute subendocardial ischemia would come with symptoms and a dynamic tracing or troponin rise; his changes are old and he has no symptoms.",
1:"Digoxin produces a scooped (\"reverse check\") ST depression, and he is not on digoxin.",
2:"An old lateral infarct would leave Q waves in I and aVL and tends to shift the axis right, not produce strain with high voltage.",
4:"Wellens T waves are deep, symmetric anterior T inversions after chest pain; his are asymmetric, lateral and unchanged for years."},
eli5="A thick heart muscle also changes how the heart 'resets' after each beat, which tilts the ST segments down in the side leads. It looks like a heart attack sign, but here it's old and stable."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_lbbbCase', cap=CH,
stem="A 76-year-old man has the electrocardiogram shown during a preoperative evaluation. The QRS duration is 150 ms.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely conduction abnormality?",
choices=["Left anterior fascicular block","Left bundle branch block","Left posterior fascicular block","Right bundle branch block","Ventricular preexcitation","Ventricular tachycardia"], key=1,
exp="A wide QRS (≥120 ms) with <b>deeply negative complexes in V1–V3</b> and <b>broad, positive (often notched) R waves in I, aVL, V5 and V6</b>, with a leftward axis, is <b>left bundle branch block</b> — the W in V1 and M in V6 of <b>WiLLiaM</b> (the LL for left bundle branch block). The whole left ventricle is activated slowly, cell to cell, from the right side. The tracing also has a long PR interval. A left bundle branch block makes ischemia very hard to read.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Left bundle branch block: QRS ≥120 ms, negative V1–V3, broad positive lateral leads (WiLLiaM), usually with a leftward axis.",
wrong={0:"Left anterior fascicular block shifts the axis left but leaves the QRS narrow.",
2:"Left posterior fascicular block shifts the axis right and leaves the QRS narrow.",
3:"Right bundle branch block gives an rSR′ (M) in V1 and slurred S waves in I and V6, the opposite pattern.",
4:"Preexcitation gives a short PR with a delta wave; this tracing has a long PR.",
5:"Ventricular tachycardia is a tachycardia; this is a sinus rhythm with a wide QRS."},
eli5="When the left bundle is blocked, the left ventricle gets its signal late, by a slow side route. The QRS widens, points down in V1 and makes a broad 'M' in the left-side leads."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_rbbbCase', cap=CH,
stem="A 55-year-old man has the electrocardiogram shown. The QRS duration is 130 ms, and the frontal axis is normal. Echocardiography shows normal valves, chamber sizes and septa.{FIG}Cardiac auscultation is most likely to show which of the following?",
choices=["Fixed splitting of the second heart sound","Paradoxical splitting of the second heart sound","Single second heart sound","Wide splitting of the second heart sound","Third heart sound at the apex"], key=3,
exp="An rSR′ (\"rabbit ears\", M) pattern in V1–V2 with slurred S waves in I and V6, a wide QRS and a normal axis is <b>right bundle branch block</b> (MaRRoW: an M in V1 and a W in V6, with the RR for right bundle branch block). The right ventricle is activated late, so it <b>finishes ejecting late and P2 is delayed</b>. That gives a <b>wide split S2</b> — heard even in expiration — that still <b>widens further on inspiration</b>, because inspiration adds venous return to the right ventricle.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right bundle branch block delays right ventricular emptying, producing a wide physiologic split of S2 that persists in expiration and widens on inspiration.",
wrong={0:"A fixed split is an atrial septal defect; his septa are normal and the split still varies with breathing.",
1:"Paradoxical splitting comes from delayed aortic closure, as in left bundle branch block.",
2:"A single S2 occurs when one component is lost (severe aortic stenosis) or when the components fuse; delayed P2 separates them instead.",
4:"A third heart sound reflects a volume-overloaded ventricle, not a conduction delay."},
eli5="The right side of his heart gets its signal late, so its valve shuts late. You hear the second heart sound split into two even when he breathes out, and more when he breathes in."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_trifas', cap=CH,
stem="A 90-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after she lost consciousness without warning while sitting at dinner. She recovered within 30 seconds. Her pulse is 64/min, and blood pressure is 138/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a laceration on the forehead and no other abnormalities. Her electrocardiogram is shown; the PR interval is 260 ms.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Carotid sinus massage","Outpatient tilt-table testing","Permanent pacemaker evaluation","Reassurance and discharge home","Start metoprolol"], key=2,
exp="The tracing shows <b>right bundle branch block</b> (rSR′ in V1), <b>left axis deviation</b> (left anterior fascicular block) and a <b>long PR interval</b>: the so-called \"trifascicular\" pattern. Two of the three fascicles are blocked and conduction in the rest is slowed, so the remaining pathway is fragile. Sudden syncope without a prodrome in an older patient with this tracing suggests <b>intermittent high-grade or complete atrioventricular block</b>, which can progress to asystole. She needs monitoring and evaluation for a <b>permanent pacemaker</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Syncope with bifascicular block (right bundle branch block plus a fascicular block) and a long PR interval suggests intermittent complete heart block; evaluate for a pacemaker.",
wrong={0:"Carotid sinus massage could provoke profound bradycardia or asystole in a patient with fragile conduction.",
1:"Tilt-table testing evaluates reflex (vasovagal) syncope, which has a prodrome; hers came without warning with a conduction-system tracing.",
3:"Unheralded syncope with advanced conduction disease is high risk; she should not be discharged without evaluation.",
4:"A beta-blocker would further slow atrioventricular conduction and could precipitate complete heart block."},
eli5="Her heart's wiring has three branches and two are already cut, with the last one slow. Fainting out of nowhere means that last branch is probably failing at times, so she likely needs a pacemaker."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_wpw8', cap=CH,
stem="A 22-year-old man comes to the office because of three episodes of sudden, rapid palpitations in the past year, each ending abruptly after about 20 minutes. He is asymptomatic now. His resting electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the short PR interval on this tracing?",
choices=["Accelerated conduction through the atrioventricular node","Atrial impulse bypassing the atrioventricular node","Ectopic atrial rhythm arising near the node","Junctional rhythm with retrograde atrial activation","Rapid sinoatrial nodal discharge"], key=1,
exp="A <b>short PR interval</b>, a <b>slurred upstroke of the QRS (delta wave)</b> and a slightly wide QRS are <b>ventricular preexcitation</b>. An accessory pathway (the bundle of Kent) connects atrium to ventricle directly and <b>bypasses the atrioventricular node's built-in delay</b>, so ventricular activation starts early, removing the isoelectric PR segment. Early activation spreads cell to cell (the delta wave) until the normal conduction system catches up. With episodes of tachycardia, this is <b>Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome</b>; the tall R waves in V1–V3 point to a left-sided pathway.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In Wolff–Parkinson–White, an accessory pathway bypasses the atrioventricular nodal delay, producing a short PR and a delta wave.",
wrong={0:"Enhanced nodal conduction (as in Lown–Ganong–Levine) can shorten the PR, but it produces no delta wave and no QRS widening.",
2:"An ectopic atrial rhythm changes the P-wave shape; it does not slur the QRS upstroke.",
3:"A junctional rhythm gives an absent or inverted P wave near the QRS, without a delta wave.",
4:"A faster sinus rate shortens the R–R interval, not the PR interval."},
eli5="An extra wire lets the signal skip the atrioventricular node's built-in pause, so the ventricle starts early. That makes the PR short and gives the QRS a sloped start, the delta wave."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_lqt', cap=CH,
stem="A 17-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after she fainted when her alarm clock rang this morning. She has had two similar episodes, one while swimming. Her paternal uncle died suddenly at age 26. Her electrocardiogram in sinus rhythm is shown; the corrected QT interval is 540 ms. Serum potassium and magnesium concentrations are normal, and she takes no medications.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of her episodes?",
choices=["Accessory pathway conduction of atrial fibrillation","Delayed afterdepolarizations from calcium overload","Early afterdepolarizations from slow repolarization","Re-entry around a ventricular scar","Vasovagal withdrawal of sympathetic tone"], key=2,
exp="Syncope triggered by a sudden noise or swimming, a family history of sudden death, and a corrected QT of 540 ms with normal electrolytes and no drugs is <b>congenital long QT syndrome</b>, usually an autosomal dominant <b>potassium-channel</b> defect. Slow repolarization (a long phase 2–3) lets sodium and calcium channels recover and reopen before the cell has repolarized, producing <b>early afterdepolarizations</b> (triggered activity). These set off <b>torsades de pointes</b>, which causes syncope (\"they just go down\") or sudden death.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Congenital long QT syndrome prolongs repolarization, allowing early afterdepolarizations that trigger torsades de pointes.",
wrong={0:"Accessory-pathway conduction of atrial fibrillation needs preexcitation (a delta wave), not a long QT.",
1:"Delayed afterdepolarizations from calcium overload occur after full repolarization, as in digoxin toxicity or ischemia.",
3:"Scar-related re-entry requires structural disease such as an old infarct; she is 17 with a long QT.",
4:"Vasovagal syncope has a prodrome and a benign family history; hers is triggered by startle with a long QT."},
eli5="Her heart cells take too long to reset after each beat. While they're slowly resetting, they can fire an extra early spark, which can start a dangerous twisting rhythm that makes her faint."),

dict(sec=7, img=None,
stem="A 71-year-old woman with paroxysmal atrial fibrillation takes dofetilide. She is admitted with gastroenteritis and given intravenous ondansetron for vomiting. Serum potassium is 3.1 mEq/L and magnesium is 1.3 mg/dL. Her corrected QT interval is 530 ms.{FIG}She is at greatest immediate risk for which of the following arrhythmias?",
choices=["Atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia","Complete heart block","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Rapid atrial fibrillation","Torsades de pointes"], key=4,
exp="Two QT-prolonging drugs are stacked — <b>dofetilide</b> (a class III potassium-channel blocker, the one drug Dr. Qazi has personally seen cause torsades) and <b>ondansetron</b> (an antiemetic) — and both electrolytes that protect repolarization are low (<b>potassium and magnesium</b>). The corrected QT is 530 ms, over the 500-ms danger line. The risk is <b>torsades de pointes</b>: polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose amplitude waxes and wanes. Stop the extra QT drugs, replete potassium and magnesium, and give intravenous magnesium if torsades occurs.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> QT-prolonging drugs plus hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, with a corrected QT over 500 ms, put patients at high risk of torsades de pointes.",
wrong={0:"Nodal re-entrant tachycardia depends on dual nodal pathways, not on repolarization time.",
1:"Complete heart block is not a typical result of class III drugs with low potassium; the danger is triggered ventricular activity.",
2:"Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia usually arises from re-entry around scar; a long QT predisposes to the polymorphic form.",
3:"Her atrial fibrillation may recur, but the immediate life-threatening risk comes from the long QT."},
eli5="She's on two medicines that both slow the heart's reset, and her potassium and magnesium, which help the reset, are low. That combination can trigger a twisting, deadly rhythm called torsades."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_highk', cap=CH,
stem="A 58-year-old man receiving hemodialysis is brought to the emergency department with profound weakness after he missed two sessions while away on a weekend trip. His pulse is 66/min, and blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg. His electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following serum abnormalities best explains these findings?",
choices=["Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hypomagnesemia"], key=1,
exp="A dialysis patient who missed sessions and is profoundly weak, with <b>tall, peaked T waves</b>, flattened or absent P waves and a <b>widened QRS</b>, has <b>hyperkalemia</b> (this man's potassium was 9.6 mEq/L). The sequence: peaked T waves first, then every interval widens (PR lengthens, QRS broadens), P waves flatten and disappear, and finally a sine-wave pattern precedes ventricular fibrillation or asystole. It is a medical emergency.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hyperkalemia produces peaked T waves first, then PR and QRS prolongation, loss of P waves and finally a sine-wave pattern.",
wrong={0:"Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval; it does not peak the T waves or widen the QRS.",
2:"Hypocalcemia prolongs the QT (a long ST segment) without peaking the T waves.",
3:"Hypokalemia flattens the T wave and brings out U waves, the reverse of this picture.",
4:"Hypomagnesemia resembles hypokalemia, with a long QT and torsades risk, not peaked T waves."},
eli5="When potassium gets very high, the T waves turn into tall sharp tents and the whole heartbeat signal gets wider. In a dialysis patient who skipped treatments, that's the classic warning sign."),

dict(sec=7, img=None,
stem="A 64-year-old man with chronic kidney disease takes lisinopril and spironolactone. His serum potassium has been rising over several weeks.{FIG}Which of the following electrocardiographic changes is expected to appear first?",
choices=["Loss of P waves","Peaked, narrow T waves","Prolonged PR interval","Sine-wave pattern","Widened QRS complex"], key=1,
exp="The first and most sensitive electrocardiographic sign of rising potassium is <b>tall, narrow, peaked T waves</b> (\"peaked\" means narrow and pointed, not merely tall). As potassium rises further, every interval widens: the <b>PR lengthens</b>, the <b>QRS broadens</b>, the <b>P wave flattens and disappears</b>, and finally the complexes merge into a <b>sine wave</b> before ventricular fibrillation or asystole. Both of his drugs (an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor and a mineralocorticoid antagonist) raise potassium, especially with kidney disease.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Peaked T waves are the earliest electrocardiographic sign of hyperkalemia, followed by PR and QRS prolongation, loss of P waves and a sine-wave pattern.",
wrong={0:"Loss of P waves comes later, after the intervals have widened.",
2:"PR prolongation follows the T-wave changes as potassium rises further.",
3:"A sine-wave pattern is preterminal, the last stage before arrest.",
4:"QRS widening appears after the T-wave peaking, at higher potassium levels."},
eli5="As potassium creeps up, the first thing you see on the ECG is sharp, tented T waves. Only later do the other waves stretch out and finally melt into a wavy line."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_hypok', cap=CH,
stem="A 22-year-old woman is admitted after 4 days of persistent vomiting. She is weak and has cramps in her legs. Her electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}She is at greatest risk for which of the following arrhythmias?",
choices=["Atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction","Mobitz II atrioventricular block","Sinus bradycardia","Torsades de pointes","Ventricular preexcitation"], key=3,
exp="Prolonged vomiting causes <b>hypokalemia</b> (hers was 1.8 mEq/L). The electrocardiogram shows <b>flat T waves with prominent U waves</b>. The U wave merges with the T, so the \"QT\" (really a QU interval) looks long. Low potassium slows repolarization and increases ventricular irritability, raising the risk of <b>early afterdepolarizations and torsades de pointes</b>, especially with low magnesium (which usually travels with it).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hypokalemia flattens T waves and produces U waves, lengthening the apparent QT and predisposing to torsades de pointes.",
wrong={0:"Atrial flutter is not a characteristic consequence of hypokalemia.",
1:"Mobitz II block is a structural His–Purkinje conduction disease, not a typical effect of low potassium.",
2:"Hypokalemia tends to increase ectopy, not slow the sinus rate.",
4:"Preexcitation requires an accessory pathway, which electrolytes do not create."},
eli5="Low potassium makes the heart's reset slow and sloppy, which shows up as a flat T wave with an extra bump (the U wave). That slow reset can trigger the twisting rhythm called torsades."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_infmi', cap=CH,
stem="A 55-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 4 hours after the onset of crushing substernal chest pain. His pulse is 58/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. His electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely responsible?",
choices=["Diagonal branch","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","Right coronary artery"], key=4,
exp="<b>ST elevation in II, III and aVF</b> with <b>reciprocal ST depression in I and aVL</b> is an <b>inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)</b>. The inferior wall is supplied by the <b>posterior descending artery, which arises from the right coronary artery</b> in about 80% of people (right dominance). The reciprocal depression confirms that the elevation is real. His slow pulse fits: the right coronary artery usually supplies the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes. In the ~20% who are left-dominant, the circumflex can produce the same picture.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Inferior STEMI (ST elevation in II, III, aVF with reciprocal depression in I and aVL) usually reflects right coronary artery occlusion.",
wrong={0:"A diagonal branch supplies the anterolateral wall (I, aVL, V5–V6), not the inferior wall.",
1:"The left anterior descending artery supplies the anterior wall and septum (V1–V4).",
2:"The circumflex supplies the lateral and posterior walls; it causes an inferior infarct only in the ~20% who are left-dominant.",
3:"Left main occlusion produces widespread ischemia (diffuse ST depression with ST elevation in aVR) and usually shock."},
eli5="The bottom (inferior) wall of the heart shows up in leads II, III and aVF. In most people the right coronary artery feeds that wall, so it's the blocked one."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_ami', cap=CH,
stem="A 63-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 10 hours of chest pain and sweating. Her pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Her electrocardiogram is shown. The nearest catheterization laboratory can open the artery within 60 minutes.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Admission and repeat troponin in 6 hours","Exercise stress testing in the morning","Primary percutaneous intervention now","Intravenous heparin and observation","Transthoracic echocardiography first"], key=2,
exp="ST elevation across V1–V6, I and aVL with reciprocal inferior ST depression is an <b>acute anterior (anterolateral) ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)</b> from the left anterior descending artery, the \"widowmaker\" that feeds more than 40% of the left ventricle. ST elevation means transmural injury, usually 100% occlusion. \"Time is muscle\": <b>open the artery now</b>. With a catheterization laboratory available within the time window, that is primary percutaneous coronary intervention (door to balloon under 90 minutes); otherwise thrombolytics then transfer.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A STEMI needs immediate reperfusion, preferably primary percutaneous coronary intervention; do not wait for troponin.",
wrong={0:"Troponin is not needed to act on a STEMI; waiting loses myocardium.",
1:"Stress testing is contraindicated in an acute coronary syndrome.",
3:"Heparin is adjunctive; it does not open an occluded artery.",
4:"Echocardiography must not delay reperfusion of a diagnosed STEMI."},
eli5="This ECG shows the front wall of her heart dying from a blocked artery. Every minute counts, so you open the artery right away in the cath lab."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_postlat', cap=CH,
stem="A 60-year-old woman comes to the emergency department 3 hours after the onset of chest pressure. Her electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the changes in leads V1 to V3?",
choices=["Anterior subendocardial ischemia","Left ventricular hypertrophy with strain","Posterior wall transmural infarction","Right bundle branch block","Right ventricular hypertrophy"], key=2,
exp="No standard lead faces the <b>posterior wall</b>, so a posterior infarct is read <b>from the front as its mirror image</b>: ST depression (the reflection of ST elevation), tall R waves (the reflection of Q waves) and upright T waves in <b>V1–V3</b>. This is an acute <b>posterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)</b>, usually from the left circumflex (or a dominant right coronary) artery, and it needs reperfusion like any STEMI. Posterior leads (V7–V9) show the ST elevation directly.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A posterior myocardial infarction appears in V1–V3 as the mirror image of injury: ST depression, tall R waves and upright T waves.",
wrong={0:"Anterior subendocardial ischemia also depresses ST segments, but it does not produce tall R waves with upright T waves in V1–V3.",
1:"Left ventricular strain causes lateral ST depression with T inversion, not anterior ST depression with tall R waves.",
3:"Right bundle branch block makes a tall R′ in V1 but requires a QRS of 120 ms or more.",
4:"Right ventricular hypertrophy gives a tall R in V1 with a right axis, without acute ST depression and chest pain."},
eli5="No ECG lead looks at the back of the heart, so a heart attack there shows up backwards in the front leads: the ST segments dip down instead of rising."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_nstemi', cap=CH,
stem="A 68-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 2 hours of chest pressure at rest. His pulse is 92/min, and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. His electrocardiogram is shown. High-sensitivity troponin is elevated.{FIG}The ST-segment changes on this tracing most likely reflect which of the following?",
choices=["Completed transmural necrosis","Normal repolarization variant","Pericardial inflammation","Subendocardial ischemic injury","Transmural injury from total occlusion"], key=3,
exp="<b>ST depression</b> means <b>subendocardial injury</b>: the inner layer, farthest from the epicardial arteries and squeezed hardest during systole, becomes ischemic when a ruptured plaque narrows the artery (70–90%) but some flow continues. With a raised troponin this is an <b>non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)</b>. ST depression is often diffuse rather than confined to one territory. Management is to stabilize first; it is not an indication for emergent revascularization as a STEMI is. ST elevation, by contrast, means transmural injury from total occlusion, and Q waves mean dead myocardium.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> ST depression reflects subendocardial ischemic injury (NSTEMI when troponin rises); ST elevation reflects transmural injury, and Q waves completed necrosis.",
wrong={0:"Completed transmural necrosis leaves pathologic Q waves, not ST depression.",
1:"A normal variant would not cause new ST depression with chest pain and a raised troponin.",
2:"Pericarditis causes diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.",
4:"Total occlusion with transmural injury causes ST elevation (STEMI)."},
eli5="When the artery is narrowed but not completely blocked, only the inner layer of heart muscle gets starved. That shows up as ST segments dipping down instead of rising."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_oldmi', cap=CH,
stem="A 53-year-old man with known ischemic heart disease has a routine electrocardiogram. He has no chest pain. The tracing is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains these findings?",
choices=["Acute inferior transmural injury","Normal Q wave confined to lead III","Old completed inferior myocardial infarction","Subendocardial ischemia of the inferior wall"], key=2,
exp="<b>Pathologic Q waves</b> (at least one small box wide) in an anatomic pattern — <b>II, III and aVF</b> — with <b>flat ST segments</b> mean <b>dead myocardium from an old, completed inferior infarction</b>; nothing acute is happening. Q waves are usually established by 12–24 hours after an infarct. A small Q wave in lead III alone can be normal, but here the Q waves are present in all three inferior leads. Inferior Q waves also explain a <b>left axis</b>, because they make aVF negative.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pathologic Q waves in contiguous leads with flat ST segments indicate an old completed infarction; inferior Q waves can produce left axis deviation.",
wrong={0:"Acute transmural injury would show ST elevation in II, III and aVF.",
1:"An isolated small Q in lead III can be normal, but Q waves in II, III and aVF form an infarct pattern.",
3:"Subendocardial ischemia produces ST depression, not Q waves with flat ST segments."},
eli5="Q waves are a scar's footprint on the ECG. Seeing them in the three bottom-wall leads with flat ST segments means an old heart attack that has healed into scar."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_hyperacute', cap='Leads V2 and V3',
stem="A 52-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after the sudden onset of severe chest pressure while shoveling snow. He is diaphoretic. His pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 148/92 mm Hg. Leads V2 and V3 of his electrocardiogram are shown. Serum potassium concentration is 4.2 mEq/L, and he has normal kidney function.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the T-wave changes?",
choices=["Early repolarization variant","Acute transmural ischemia","Hyperkalemia","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Resolving pericarditis"], key=1,
exp="Within minutes of a coronary occlusion, the first electrocardiographic change is <b>hyperacute T waves</b>: <b>broad-based, tall and symmetric</b> T waves in the affected territory (here the anterior leads, the left anterior descending artery). They precede ST elevation, which is followed by Q waves and T-wave inversion. The tall T waves can be confused with hyperkalemia, but his potassium is normal and hyperkalemic T waves are <b>narrow and peaked</b>. Repeat the tracing every few minutes; he is evolving an anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hyperacute T waves — broad, tall and symmetric — are the earliest sign of coronary occlusion, preceding ST elevation; hyperkalemic T waves are narrow and peaked.",
wrong={0:"Early repolarization raises the J point in young, asymptomatic people; it does not appear suddenly with chest pain.",
2:"Hyperkalemia produces narrow, peaked T waves; his potassium is normal.",
3:"Left ventricular hypertrophy produces high QRS voltage and lateral strain, not new broad anterior T waves.",
4:"Pericarditis produces diffuse ST elevation with PR depression, and resolving pericarditis inverts T waves."},
eli5="In the first minutes of a blocked artery, the T waves swell up big and broad, even before the ST segments rise. It's the earliest warning sign of a heart attack."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_peri', cap=CH,
stem="A 27-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 2 days of sharp chest pain that worsens when he lies down and breathes in and improves when he sits forward. He had an upper respiratory infection 1 week ago. His temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), pulse is 98/min, and blood pressure is 124/76 mm Hg. His electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Acute anterior myocardial infarction","Acute pericarditis","Brugada syndrome","Early repolarization","Left ventricular aneurysm","Pulmonary embolism","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"], key=1,
exp="Pleuritic chest pain relieved by sitting forward after a viral illness, with <b>diffuse</b> concave ST elevation (in many territories rather than one) and <b>PR-segment depression</b>, is <b>acute pericarditis</b>. An ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) is confined to one coronary territory with reciprocal depression in the opposite leads; pericarditis is everywhere, and its only reciprocal changes are in aVR (and V1). The history settles it — was the patient symptomatic, and how?<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pericarditis causes diffuse ST elevation with PR depression, not confined to a coronary territory, with pleuritic positional pain.",
wrong={0:"An anterior STEMI is territorial (V1–V4) with reciprocal inferior depression; his elevation is diffuse with PR depression.",
2:"Brugada syndrome shows coved ST elevation in V1–V2 only, without pleuritic pain.",
3:"Early repolarization is an asymptomatic finding in young people without PR depression or chest pain.",
4:"A left ventricular aneurysm causes persistent ST elevation with Q waves after an old infarct.",
5:"Pulmonary embolism causes sinus tachycardia and right heart strain (S1Q3T3, right bundle branch block), not diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.",
6:"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy follows emotional stress in older women and mimics an anterior STEMI with apical ballooning; it does not cause PR depression or positional pain."},
eli5="Inflamed heart lining irritates the whole surface, so the ST segments rise in almost every lead, not just one area. The sharp pain that eases when leaning forward fits too."),

dict(sec=7, img=None,
stem="A healthy 22-year-old college sprinter has a pre-participation electrocardiogram. He has no symptoms and no family history of sudden death. The tracing shows 2-mm elevation of the J point with a small notch at the end of the QRS complex in V2 to V4, concave ST segments, and no reciprocal ST depression. A tracing from 3 years ago is identical.{FIG}The electrocardiographic finding is best explained by which of the following?",
choices=["Coved ST elevation from a cardiac sodium channel defect","Diffuse inflammation of the visceral pericardium","Larger epicardial transient outward potassium current","Subepicardial ischemia from coronary vasospasm","Transmural injury from coronary occlusion"], key=2,
exp="J-point elevation with notching and concave ST segments in a young, asymptomatic athlete, stable over years and without reciprocal change, is <b>early repolarization</b>, usually a normal variant. The lecture's mechanism: the <b>epicardium has a larger transient outward potassium current (Ito)</b> than the endocardium, so early in repolarization the two layers sit at different voltages, and that gradient lifts the J point. The history and old tracings keep it from being called a heart attack.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Early repolarization — J-point elevation with notching in young, asymptomatic people — reflects a larger epicardial transient outward potassium current and is usually benign.",
wrong={0:"A sodium-channel defect produces Brugada syndrome: coved ST elevation in V1–V2 with T inversion and a risk of sudden death.",
1:"Pericarditis causes diffuse ST elevation with PR depression and pleuritic pain.",
3:"Vasospasm causes transient ST elevation with chest pain, not a stable tracing over 3 years.",
4:"Coronary occlusion causes territorial ST elevation with symptoms and reciprocal depression."},
eli5="In some young, fit people, the outer layer of heart muscle resets a little faster than the inner layer. That small mismatch nudges the start of the ST segment up, and it's harmless."),

dict(sec=7, img='B_chbStemi', cap=CH,
stem="A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the onset of chest pain and light-headedness. Her pulse is 40/min, and blood pressure is 86/52 mm Hg. Her electrocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains her slow heart rate?",
choices=["Excess vagal tone from the Bezold–Jarisch reflex alone","Right coronary ischemia of the atrioventricular node","Necrosis of the left bundle branch from septal infarction","Rupture of the posteromedial papillary muscle","Sinus node arrest from left circumflex occlusion"], key=1,
exp="Inferior ST elevation with reciprocal depression in aVL is an <b>inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)</b>, usually from the <b>right coronary artery</b>. The P waves and QRS complexes are independent: <b>complete (third-degree) heart block</b>. The <b>atrioventricular nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 90%</b> of people, so inferior infarction commonly causes nodal ischemia and heart block. Block from an inferior infarct is often at the node, with a narrow escape rhythm, and frequently resolves with reperfusion; temporary pacing supports her meanwhile.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Inferior STEMI from right coronary artery occlusion can cause complete heart block because the right coronary artery usually supplies the atrioventricular node.",
wrong={0:"Vagal reflexes contribute to bradycardia in inferior infarction, but they do not dissociate P waves from QRS complexes.",
2:"Septal (anterior) infarction can block the bundle branches, but her infarct is inferior.",
3:"Papillary muscle rupture causes acute mitral regurgitation and pulmonary edema, not heart block.",
4:"Sinus arrest would remove the P waves; here they continue regularly but fail to conduct."},
eli5="Her heart attack is in the bottom wall, fed by the right coronary artery. That same artery feeds the atrioventricular node, the relay station, so the relay has stopped passing signals through."),
]
